<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Melayu Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">645 CE – 686 CE (approx.) · capital Jambi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Buddhist kingdom on the Batang Hari River in Jambi, Sumatra, which gave its name to the Malay people before being absorbed by Srivijaya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chinese records note an embassy from Mo-lo-yu in 644 or 645. The kingdom lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batang-hari-river">Batang Hari River</a>, centred near modern Jambi, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muaro-jambi">Muara Jambi</a> temple complex later grew up. It controlled gold from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> highlands and access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a> stopped at Malayu on his voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 671, staying two months on his way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> before sailing on to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah</a>. Little else is known of its rulers or institutions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a> returned in the 680s he wrote that Malayu had become part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. The undated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karang-brahi-inscription">Karang Brahi inscription</a>, found upriver in Jambi and usually placed close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kapur-inscription">Kota Kapur inscription</a> of 686, records Srivijaya's authority in the area. The name Malayu revived later in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dharmasraya">Dharmasraya</a> kingdom and spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-language">Malay language</a> and people.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/melayu-kingdom.jpg" alt="Melayu Kingdom" width="320" height="511"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malay_Kingdoms_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>645 CE – 686 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jambi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malayu, Mo-lo-yu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=666&amp;focus=melayu-kingdom" aria-label="Melayu Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7557 2239 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Melayu Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7877 2495L7833 2434"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7877 2495h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7877 2495h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jambi. <a href="/atlas?year=666&amp;focus=melayu-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

